emy of Sciences. Only Tulane University Herbarium (NO) remains a distinct collection.&nbsp;&nbsp;Geographic distribution is global, but strengths are Louisiana and the southeastern USA, southen California, Colorado Rocky Mountains, Yucatan Peninsula, Colombia and Brazil. Families well-represented include Asteraceae, Fabaceae, Cyperaceae, Rubiaceae, and Poaceae. Significant collectors include: Fr. G. Ars&egrave;ne, L. M. Booth,&nbsp;C. A. Brown,&nbsp;I. W. Clokey, R. S. Cocks, D. Demaree,&nbsp;J. A. Ewan,&nbsp;A. Featherman,&nbsp;A. Gray, J. Hale, J. F. Joor, D. D. Keck, E. P. Killip, J. G. Lemmon, Y. E. J. Mexia, F. W. Peirson,&nbsp;N. F. Peterson,&nbsp;J. L. Riddell, and D. Stone,&nbsp;R. D. Thomas, S. C. Tucker, L. E. Urbatsch, I. V&eacute;lez, and E. C. Wurzlow.","logo":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/content/collicon/lsu.jpg","url":"http://www.herbarium.lsu.edu/","emllink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=210","archivelink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/content/dwca/lsu-vascularplants_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"jkluse@lsu.edu"},{"last_name":"Jennifer S. Kluse, Collections Manager","email":"jkluse@lsu.edu"},{"last_name":"Jennifer S. Kluse, Collections Manager","email":"jkluse@lsu.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC3 BY-NC","publisher":"42f2a0a4-5981-4874-88d5-9efb1e8d52c9","totalCount":316835,"hitRatio":0.000006312433916707435},{"uuid":"664bd710-8791-4dba-a3b6-000a1b140951","itemCount":1,"name":"Pittsburg State University, Theodore M. Sperry Herbarium","description":"Specimens of the Sperry Herbarium are used in support of teaching several botany courses, outreach, and reasearch by undergraduates, graduates, and facutly. Its 55,244 specimens document the distribution and natural variation of vascular plants and bryophytes, primarily from Kansas and adjacent states, North America, ahd 60 foreign countries. Approximately 13,350 new collections have been added since 2014, mostly comprising student collections. Approximately 1400 vascular plant specimens are backlogged from North America, about 300 from Madagascar and hundreds of bryophytes. Many specimens are unicates. Important holdings from abroad include the Democratic Republic of the Congo (T. M. Sperry), Peru (H. Tuomisto), Myrtaceae (H.S. MacKee and others from New Caledonia); Eugenia and Syzygium from Madagascar, Poaceae (worldwide), and 30 isotypes. Data for some 7500 bryophytes are housed here or at the Consortium of Bryophyte Herbaria (https://bryophyteportal.org), including ca. 250 from Peru of Timme. Some collections of fungi and algae used primarily in teaching will be datasased in the future. Phenological data (flowering/fruiting) and geocoordinates are present for 47 percent of the specimens, and over 15 percent are vouchers for published research. The top in-house collectors numerically are W.W. Holland, S.Y. Pryer, T.M. Sperry, S.L. Timme, M. Daines and N. Snow. Student and volunteer curatorial assistance, and financial support from the PSU College of Arts and Sciences and the National Science Foundation (CSBR 1756276) are gratefully acknowledged. Homepage: https://www.pittstate.edu/biology/the-sperry-herbarium.html&nbsp;","logo":"https://swbiodiversity.org/seinet/content/collicon/ksp.png","url":"http://www.pittstate.edu/department/herbarium/index.dot","emllink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=177","archivelink":"https://ngpherbaria.org/portal/content/dwca/ksp_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"nsnow@pittstate.edu"},{"last_name":"Neil Snow","email":"nsnow@pittstate.edu"},{"last_name":"Neil Snow","email":"nsnow@pittstate.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC4 BY-NC","publisher":"d6f2ff48-a362-4406-93ab-fdf7f140253b","totalCount":48519,"hitRatio":0.000020610482491395125},{"uuid":"83ad8494-136b-485a-87d4-8ce01dd6a8de","itemCount":1,"name":"University of South Carolina, A. C. Moore Herbarium Vascular Plant Collection","description":"The A. C. Moore Herbarium is an important part of the Department of Biological Sciences at the University of South Carolina (Columbia Campus). Founded in 1907 by Dr. Andrew Charles Moore, the original collection of dried plant specimens is now part of an ever-growing collection. Total holdings are just over 130,000 specimens, making the A. C. Moore Herbarium the largest in the state of South Carolina. Researchers and visitors will find a diverse collection of vascular and nonvascular plant material primarily from the Southeastern United States and more specifically from South Carolina. Now over 100 years old, the A. C. Moore Herbarium continues to be an indispensable resource for botanical knowledge. Note to users: Some data fields contain HTML formatting. Please contact us for assistance if this presents an issue with its use.","logo":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/content/collicon/usch.jpg","url":"http://www.herbarium.org","emllink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=147","archivelink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/content/dwca/usch_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"first_name":"Herrick","last_name":"Brown","role":"Herbarium Curator","email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"first_name":"Avery","last_name":"Browning","role":"Assistant Curator","email":"averyob@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"first_name":"Csilla","last_name":"Czako","role":"GIS Specialist/SCDNR NHT Botany and Plant Conservation Liason","email":"czakoc@dnr.sc.edu"},{"first_name":"Herrick","last_name":"Brown","role":"Herbarium Curator","email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"first_name":"Avery","last_name":"Browning","role":"Assistant Curator","email":"averyob@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"first_name":"Csilla","last_name":"Czako","role":"GIS Specialist/SCDNR NHT Botany and Plant Conservation Liason","email":"czakoc@dnr.sc.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC0","publisher":"42f2a0a4-5981-4874-88d5-9efb1e8d52c9","totalCount":125741,"hitRatio":0.000007952855472757494},{"uuid":"910eadc8-8131-428c-b28a-91d0e2890f1d","itemCount":1,"name":"Delaware State University, Claude E. Phillips Herbarium","description":"The Claude E. Phillips Herbarium (DOV) at Delaware State University is the only public herbarium on the Delmarva Peninsula (USA) and houses over 200,000 specimens of vascular plants, bryophytes, lichens, fungi, and algae. DOV&rsquo;s collections span more than 200 years of botanical exploration in Delmarva, the neighboring states, and other parts of the U.S. As a regional herbarium, DOV often surprises with its significant historical collection that is worldwide in scope. Ancillary collections of bark; botanical beads; cones, fruits and seeds; dried botanicals; fibers; fossils; potpourri ingredients; wood; and permanent slides augment DOV&rsquo;s holdings.","logo":"https://midatlanticherbaria.org/portal/content/collicon/dov.jpg","url":"https://herbarium.desu.edu/","emllink":"https://sernecportal.org/portal/collections/datasets/emlhandler.php?collid=334","archivelink":"https://midatlanticherbaria.org/portal/content/dwca/dov_dwc-a.zip","contacts":[{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"hbrown@mailbox.sc.edu"},{"email":"chwa@desu.edu"},{"last_name":"Cynthia Hong-Wa","email":"chwa@desu.edu"},{"last_name":"Cynthia Hong-Wa","email":"chwa@desu.edu"}],"data_rights":"CC4 BY-NC","publisher":"25b6b373-8115-43ee-9fee-a0e1a7262d7f","totalCount":174046,"hitRatio":0.0000057456074830791855}],"aggs":{"rs":{"doc_count_error_upper_bound":0,"sum_other_doc_count":0,"buckets":[{"key":"4e3043a6-d48a-4a35-b5fb-f67d50cbc158","doc_count":9},{"key":"92e4e092-6dcb-46bc-85a0-dea8310aba45","doc_count":8},{"key":"ddef79ec-043c-4027-9876-c4a298feff6d","doc_count":4},{"key":"120d557c-c5be-474d-98f0-1ba00ae16b40","doc_count":2},{"key":"4523e216-ee13-4b15-a3f7-a6fd56431604","doc_count":2},{"key":"664bd710-8791-4dba-a3b6-000a1b140